## [codex] 2026-10-11 - R1 steel-man, critique and next step

Boss resumed this task and authorized continuing with Claude in the background. Separate viewer request is dropped. This task remains design-only; Unity source directories stay untouched. The interrupted Codex reveal was absent on disk, so it is posted above without changing the sealed draft.

Steel-man of Claude's position: The richest owned content is humanoid motion, so the game should offer an interesting decision inside every exchange. Height, timing and an interrupt/guard/throw relationship could make a reused rig feel expressive without a new enemy model. Connecting clean melee reads to an elemental opportunity joins short-range and ranged play into one rhythm. Funding settlement changes gives that mastery a visible purpose. His three axes distinguish immediate competence, institutional access and place improvement; each can reward a different player preference. All-human adversaries can be a coherent setting decision rather than an apology for a missing creature pack.

Practical strongest case: With one opponent in a quiet arena, varied high/body/low tells, deliberate guard transitions and generous novice timing, a height rule could teach players to look at the moving body instead of an icon. A visible elemental meter can carry the longer exchange rhythm. A restored stall or gate could introduce a new training opponent or preparation choice, linking civic change to fighting skill. These are concrete hypotheses supported by available art ingredients.

Counterarguments and limits: (1) Matching reaction clips demonstrate presentation coverage, not that the camera and tell timing make three-height defense legible. Selection of guard height while moving/targeting needs an explicit input and delay. (2) Triangle outcomes need definitions for throw range, attempt tell, escape, mutual attempts, wall obstruction and crowded encounters; paired clips alone do not provide them. (3) Four sites, three enemies at once, two elements including chain targeting, three progression axes and rebuilt houses multiply dependencies before we know if one fight works. (4) Element charge earned only from melee may remove the caster role rather than integrate it; decide whether casting is a viable choice from the first encounter. (5) A new hero/audio module or generated production content is outside the currently downloaded-content canvas; document gaps and existing-rig/primitive-UI fallbacks, rather than assume those sources.

