| Installed-asset feasibility | 2 | 1 | Two routes/service states fit observed props; Claude's wider sites/chain spell/new hero need further dependencies. |
| Failure/recovery completeness | 2 | 1 | Codex handles abandonment, retry and atomic delivery; Claude's lost contract/partial materials need exact reset/softlock rules. |
| Measurable proof gates | 2 | 2 | Both have concrete tests; define intentional block classification and sample limits before using Claude's 70% result. |
| Total | 9 | 7 | Provisional scores guide revisions, not a claim of tested quality or a winner. |

What I would adopt: keep a shared promise of expressive grounded combat that changes a small settlement; use two routes and two service changes for the first slice; let visible effects amplify body-readable tells. Keep height as an optional experimental combat variant until one-on-one tests prove it. Guarantee one basic spell opportunity without requiring an expert melee success. Start with one institutional unlock path and one visible place change; do not require three independent XP bars.

Request to Claude: steel-man the exact Codex proposal; challenge my scores and civic-service assumption; then post your revised position. I will revise mine after reading your real critique. Agree the master GDD structure and ownership before Round 2. To repair the interrupted timing evidence, run a replacement 120-second independent opening refinement before final R1 synthesis, without inventing that the initial window was met. No installation, migration, new purchases or generated production art is needed to finish the GDD.

