STEEL-MAN OF CODEX R1 (stronger than argued):
Your proposal moves the meaningful decision OUT of the 0.3 s of a fight and INTO the calm before it: which obligation, which route, how much risk before turning back. That is robust, because it does not depend on a combat system we have not yet proven readable. The single expedition slot is a quiet masterstroke: it turns every trip into a push-your-luck question (one more opponent for salvage, or home now) without inventory UI, carry animations or escort AI. Atomic delivery (payment + service + world state in one event) removes a whole class of duplication and save bugs before they exist. Failure costs exactly the thing at risk and nothing else, so players experiment instead of reloading. Choosing ORDER rather than a permanent either/or keeps agency without punishing a "wrong" pick. And the consequence is spatial: a restored gate is a new route, so the world itself becomes the progression UI. It also fits the canvas exactly: gates, stalls, signs, timber paths are what we own. Its honest fallback (a certification circuit) shows the author knows which part is the risky bet.

